\section1 UI

For the \c UserInterface we want
\list
\li a Player Healthbar
\li the dungeon level indicator
\li the Player stats
\li a give up option
\endlist

\section3 Player Healthbar

We first start with the \c PlayerHealthBar. \br
\c ui/PlayerHealthBar.qml
\quotefile PlayerHealthBar.qml
\br

Because we have multiple UI elements we will create a \c UserInterface class where we put all the elements.\br
We can already create that with the \c PlayerHealthBar. \br
\c ui/UserInterface.qml
\code
import QtQuick
import Felgo
import QtQuick.Controls

Item {
    id: userInterface
    property int playerMaxHealth
    property int playerCurrentHealth

    anchors.margins: 12

    Item {
        id: toprow
        anchors.top: parent.top
        anchors.left: parent.left
        anchors.right: parent.right
        height: 16

        PlayerHealthBar {
            id: playerHealthBar
            anchors.top: parent.top
            anchors.left: parent.left
            anchors.bottom: parent.bottom
            max: userInterface.playerMaxHealth
            value: userInterface.playerCurrentHealth
            width: parent.width / 3
        }
    }
}
\endcode
\br

The \c UserInterface only needs to be added to the \c DungeonScene with the bindings for the player health.\br
\c scenes/DungeonScene.qml
\code
Scene {
    id: dungeonScene

    ...

    Item {
        ...
    }

    ...

    UserInterface {
        id: userInterface
        anchors.fill: parent
        playerMaxHealth: playerManager.maxHealth
        playerCurrentHealth: playerManager.currentHealth
    }

    ...
}
\endcode

Here it is important to add the \c UserInterface after the rest of content, so it will be visually on top.\br

With that in place, you can try it out. You should now be able to see the player’s health, and it will decrease whenever the player is hit by an enemy.\br
\br

\image Player_Health.png
\br

\section3 Dungeon Level Indicator

The dungeon indicator is really simple. We add it to the \c UserInterface and connect it with the dungeon level property from the \c DungeonScene. \br
\c ui/UserInterface.qml
\code
Item {
    id: userInterface
    
    property int currentDungeonLevel
    ...

    Item {
        id: toprow

        ...

        AppText {
            anchors.centerIn: parent
            anchors.horizontalCenter: parent.horizontalCenter
            text: "Level " + currentDungeonLevel
            color: "white"
            font.family: "PixelOperator8"
            font.pixelSize: 12
        }
    }
}
\endcode
\br
\br
\c scenes/DungeonScene.qml
\code
Scene {
    id: dungeonScene

    ...

    UserInterface {
        ...
        currentDungeonLevel: dungeonScene.currentDungeonLevel
        ...
    }

    ...
}
\endcode
\br

\image Dungeon_Level.png
\br

\section3 Player Stats

To implement the player stats we need to get those from the \c PlayerManager. \br
\c entities/PlayerManager.qml
\code
EntityManager {
    id: playerManager

    ...

    function playerStats() {
        return [
                {"name": "Damage","value":playerManager.damage}
                ]
    }

    ...
}
\endcode
\br
For now, these will only include the damage value, but we will extend them with additional stats later.\br
\br
To display the player stats we will add a panel.\br
\c ui/PlayerStatus.qml
\quotefile PlayerStatus.qml
\br

To not always display the stats we need to implement a button which toggles the panel visibility.\br
Then add the player stats panel to the \c UserInterface and connect it.\br
\c ui/UserInterface.qml
\code
Item {
    id: userInterface
    
    ...

    signal requestPlayerStats()

    ...

    function showPlayerStatus(playerStats) {
        playerStatus.show(playerStats);
    }

    function closePanels() {
        playerStatus.visible = false;
    }

    Item {
        id: toprow

        ...

        Row {
            anchors.top: parent.top
            anchors.right: parent.right
            height: parent.height
            spacing: 6

            MouseArea {
                id: playerStatusButton
                width: parent.height
                height: parent.height
                onClicked: playerStatus.visible ? playerStatus.visible = false : userInterface.requestPlayerStats()

                Rectangle {
                    anchors.fill: parent
                    color: "#505050"
                    border.width: 1
                    border.color: "#707070"
                }

                Image {
                    anchors.centerIn: parent
                    width: parent.width * 0.8
                    height: parent.height * 0.8
                    smooth: false
                    source: "../../assets/knight.png"
                }

                PlayerStatus {
                    id: playerStatus
                    visible: false
                    anchors.top: parent.bottom
                    anchors.right: parent.right
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
\endcode
\br
\c scenes/DungeonScene.qml
\code
Scene {
    id: dungeonScene

    ...

    onVisibleChanged: {
        if (visible) {
            ...
        } else {
            ...
            userInterface.closePanels()
        }
    }

    ...

    UserInterface {
        ...
        onRequestPlayerStats: {
            userInterface.showPlayerStatus(playerManager.playerStats());
        }
        ...
    }

    ...
}
\endcode
\br

\image Player_Stats.png
\br

\section3 Give Up Menu

Now the only thing left in the UI is the give up option.\br
For that we will implement a \c GiveUpMenu. \br
\c ui/GiveUpMenu.qml
\quotefile GiveUpMenu.qml
\br

This one will also be added to the \c UserInterface and opened with a button and then connected.\br
\c ui/UserInterface.qml
\code
Item {
    id: userInterface
    
    ...

    signal giveUp()

    ...

    Item {
        id: toprow

        ...

        Row {
            ...

            MouseArea {
                id: playerStatusButton
                ...
            }

            MouseArea {
                id: giveUpButton
                width: parent.height
                height: parent.height
                onClicked: giveUpMenu.visible = true

                Rectangle {
                    anchors.fill: parent
                    color: "#505050"
                    border.width: 1
                    border.color: "#707070"
                }

                Image {
                    anchors.centerIn: parent
                    width: parent.width * 0.6
                    height: parent.height * 0.6
                    smooth: false
                    source: "../../assets/skull.png"
                }
            }
        }
    }

    GiveUpMenu {
        id: giveUpMenu
        visible: false
        anchors.centerIn: parent
        width: 200
        height: 100
        onGiveUp: userInterface.giveUp()
    }
}
\endcode
\br
\c scenes/DungeonScene.qml
\code
Scene {
    id: dungeonScene

    ...

    UserInterface {
        ...
        onGiveUp: dungeonScene.playerDied()
        ...
    }

    ...
}
\endcode
\br

\image Give_Up_Menu.png
\br

And with that the UI is all set up.\br